 Today's newsletter is 811 words, a 3-minute read. 

 📉 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed down 0.3%.

 Oil prices wavered on conflicting message s over a deal to restore flows through the Strait of Hormuz, and investors await tomorrow's inflation report. 🔥 Today's stock spotlight: CoreWeave shares jumped over 10% in extended trading as the AI infrastructure provider reported bigger-than-expected revenue growth in Q2.


 
 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 
 
1 big thing: CAVA grows traffic, sales
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 Photo: Kevin Carter/Getty Images


 
 &nbsp; 
 
 With food-safety concerns swirling , CAVA CEO Brett Schulman says the industry must hold suppliers to high standards and adhere to rock-solid safety protocols.

 Why it matters: Recent outbreaks of salmonella and cyclospora have sickened thousands of Americans, calling attention to the vulnerability of the food supply chain.

 📈 Driving the news: Schulman acknowledged today in an Axios interview that "there's just broader concerns around leafy greens" that temporarily dampened CAVA sales in recent weeks — but he said traffic is bouncing back and that the Mediterranean fast-casual chain's food has not been affected.

 In its second quarter — which ended around the time the cyclospora outbreak began in July — CAVA showed significant growth, delivering a 9% same-store sales increase . That was higher than the 7.2% growth expected by S&amp;P Global Capital IQ. Customer traffic at comparable restaurants grew 5.3%. 😬 The big picture: CAVA's stock is down 16% over the last month as investors fret about customers shying away from vegetables.

 Schulman confirmed that CAVA sources certain products from Taylor Farms — the supplier of the since-recalled iceberg lettuce linked to the cyclospora outbreak — but said "we do not source any ingredients from Taylor Farms-impacted facilities or farms, and we do not source from Taylor Farms Mexico," the source of the iceberg lettuce recall on July 17. The bottom line: The safety concerns show why "it's more important than ever that the restaurant industry — working with the supply chain along with our governmental partners — continue to drive clear and transparent communication to restore that trust and strengthen that trust with guests," Schulman said.

