2. Bumble rewrites rules
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios


 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Men can now make the first move on Bumble, ending the longstanding rule that only women can initiate a conversation on the dating app.

 Why it matters: Bumble has been struggling to get its groove back in the ever-evolving dating landscape , with its stock down 56% over the last year.

 State of play: Bumble — which is under pressure to win back Gen Zers who are exhausted with dating apps — said today that it's making the change in part to appease younger folks.

 Many women "appreciate the flexibility for men to start the conversation," the company said in a statement, citing internal research. Zoom in: The move comes after Bumble CEO Whitney Wolfe Herd said in May on "The Axios Show" that the company would eliminate the swipe feature and hinted at a shift away from the women-must-message-first rule.

 "We will not force one gender over another to do something first," Wolfe Herd told Axios' Sara Fischer. 💭 Nathan's thought bubble: Love isn't a one-way street, so this makes some sense — but Bumble is risking losing its competitive differentiation.


 
 
